QUESTION 2: HOLIDAY SERVICE (Low-Latency C++ SDK & Aggregator)\
================================================================================\
Problem Statement:\
Design a Holiday Service exposing `getData(countryCode, year)` returning all public \
holidays. Many internal client applications access it through a shared C++ adapter. \
The service fetches data from an external/lower-level service that is slow (150ms). \
Target latency for clients is < 5ms.\
\
Stage 01: Clarify\
- Functional Reqs: Return holiday list with date, name, and observed status. \
  Support historical years and future schedules. Transform date format (MM/DD -> YYYY/MM/DD).\
- Non-Functional Reqs: End-to-end latency < 5ms for clients; 99.99% availability; \
  graceful fallback if the upstream service experiences an outage.\
- Out of Scope: Scheduling calendar events, managing national calendar laws.\
\
Stage 02: Interactions (APIs)\
- Client SDK Interface: `std::vector<Holiday> getData(const std::string& countryCode, int year);`\
- Service REST API: GET /v1/holidays?country=\{countryCode\}&year=\{year\}\
- Response: 200 OK: `\{"country": "US", "year": 2027, "holidays": [\{"date": "2027-01-01", "name": "New Year's Day"\}]\}`\
\
Stage 03: Data Model\
- holidays: id (PK), country_code (CHAR(2)), year (INT), holiday_date (DATE), \
  name (VARCHAR), is_observed (BOOL), last_updated (TIMESTAMP)\
- Unique Index: (country_code, year, holiday_date)\
\
Stage 04: Simple Design\
- Client App -> C++ Adapter Library -> Holiday Service -> Upstream External API\
\
Stage 05: Challenge & Improve\
- Multi-Tier Caching Architecture:\
  * Tier 1 (Client-Side C++ In-Memory Cache): Cache results directly in local process RAM \
    inside the C++ library (LRU Cache with 24-hour TTL). Latency: < 10 microseconds.\
  * Tier 2 (Distributed Redis Cache at Service Layer): Shared across all Holiday Service instances.\
- Precomputation / Background Sync: Holidays change rarely. A daily cron worker fetches \
  and precomputes all countries for (current_year, current_year + 1) and warms Redis.\
- Resilience: If upstream service goes down, serve stale cached data with fallback header.\
\
